    <h2>The Rise of AI Living Directly on Your Phone</h2>
    <div class="post-meta">August 1, 2026</div>
    <img src="images/photo1.jpg" alt="Code and technology">
    
    <p>For years, AI features on phones — like voice assistants or photo enhancement — worked by sending your data to a cloud server, processing it there, and sending back a result. That's changing fast.</p>
    
    <p>Modern phones now pack dedicated AI chips (called <strong>NPUs — Neural Processing Units</strong>) that can run surprisingly capable AI models directly on the device. No internet connection needed, no data leaving your phone.</p>
    
    <p>This matters for three big reasons: <strong>privacy</strong> (your data stays local), <strong>speed</strong> (no network lag), and <strong>offline access</strong> (works without signal). Companies are now racing to shrink powerful AI models small enough to fit on a chip the size of a fingernail — without losing much capability.</p>
    
    <p>It's a quiet shift, but one that's reshaping how phones are designed from the inside out.</p>
  </article>

